SM(576,{"n":"1 Thessalonians 4","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Thessalonians 4.mp3","d":2440.8,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.96,"Okay, we'll go ahead and get started when we get started."],[24.32,"And here in just a moment we'll be picking up in St."],[26.64,"Paul's first epistle to the Thessalonians, chapter four, verse one."],[32.88,"We don't have a ton of catch up to do."],[35.92,"I think we'll get some of it in stream, because Saint St."],[40.08,"Paul here is gonna in his We're getting to sort of his closing."],[44.48,"This is the the the beginning of the end."],[47.44,"As you can see, verse one of chapter four begins with Finally then."],[52.24,"So um, but much like let us complete our prayer unto the Lord, there's still a couple chapters, but there's still some time left here."],[59.84,"But it's the beginning of the end"],[62.12,"Um so he's going to refer back uh to some of the things he's been talking about."],[69.4,"Um, but most of what we've seen St."],[72.36,"Paul talking about so far, this has been one of St."],[74.84,"Paul's more positive letters uh to the Christian community in Thessalonica."],[81.0,"Um we've seen like St."],[83.32,"Paul's letters to the Corinthians didn't go quite this well."],[86.36,"Uh this is more like his epistle to the Philippians, where good things are happening there, and so it's a more positive report."],[94.52,"Uh he's gotten a report back from Timothy."],[96.52,"He's talked about how he's wanted to go visit uh Thessalonica again, and but he hadn't been able to be back there."],[102.28,"As we talked about, St."],[103.32,"Paul's writing this around 50 AD from Corinth back to Thessalonica, but he hasn't been there since he first visited."],[112.72,"Though he has sent Timothy, who's given him this good report."],[117.6,"And the quick summary beyond that has been that he's been encouraging them to sort of continue to walk in the way that he showed them when he was there."],[128.24,"Not just in what he taught them, but in how he lived his life while he was there, how they've seen Timothy living to sort of continue, uh continue on that same path that they're on."],[140.32,"Sort of a keep up the good work."],[142.52,"If things are going well, keep it up, don't get distracted, don't go off course."],[147.32,"Um and he's sort of still talking about that here. uh, as we'll see as we begin in uh chapter four"],[154.44,"Um so unless anybody has any questions or comments or abbreviations or clever acronyms they've come up with that they'd like to share, uh, we'll go ahead and uh pick up in St."],[169.96,"Paul's first epistle to the Thessalonians, chapter four, verse one."],[176.36,"Finally, then, brethren, we urge and exhort in the Lord Jesus, that you should abound more and more, just as you receive from us how you walk ought to walk and to please God."],[187.96,"So he's sort of summarizing what he has just been talking about in the past chapter and a half or so."],[194.88,"So just as you've been doing, that you would continue to abound more and more."],[200.16,"More of this."],[201.42,"More of the good stuff, less of the bad stuff."],[205.58,"Verse 2, for you know what commandments we gave you through the Lord Jesus."],[213.1,"Note, Saint Paul gave them commandments."],[219.71,"This is another connection to what we saw in the Gospels."],[222.59,"Remember, St. Matthew's Gospel ends when Christ sends them out, he says, make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, teaching them all that I have commanded you."],[235.63,"Commandments. And as Christ says, elsewhere, if you love me, you will keep my commandments."],[241.35,"Um, this is just sort of a last note."],[243.83,"We're we're past the epistles where St."],[246.47,"Paul is mainly talking about the law, but just to hammer that point home some more, when St."],[252.63,"Paul goes to a church that's full of Gentiles."],[257.13,"These are former pagans, who are now Christians, he teaches them commandments."],[265.15,"He doesn't just tell them, here are the things you need to believe, and then you'll be fine."],[271.17,"Uh, he's teaching them commandments, and we're gonna get more detail on that here in a minute."],[276.13,"Some specific commandments that he has taught them."],[284.49,"Verse 3, 4, which is really because."],[288.51,"So that because means he's expanding on what he was just talking about."],[292.48,"So the commandments he's talking about."],[295.6,"This is the will of God, your sanctification, that you should abstain from sexual immorality."],[303.92,"So the will of God, what God desires, what God is working toward in them is their sanctification, meaning them becoming holy, them being holy and set apart."],[317.64,"Meaning different from their neighbors in Thessalonica who are still pagans."],[324.7,"They need to be holy and set apart."],[326.78,"And the particular area that St."],[329.02,"Paul is focusing on here."],[330.64,"Remember, part a big part of the problems in Corinth was on the idolatry side."],[336.32,"There was some sexual immorality too."],[337.84,"I mean, Corinth had everything going on."],[339.61,"But we talked about then how sort of the two paradigmatic sins that were going on out in the nations, as as Judeans saw it, were like the two top-of-the-heap sins were sexual immorality and idolatry."],[355.31,"And all through the Old Testament, those two things are closely connected."],[358.99,"And they're very closely connected in pagan life, because, for example, pagan worship and pagan festivals often involve sexual immorality."],[366.77,"These were these were connected."],[367.81,"Yeah Yeah. So we have, as we've seen in so many ways, we have, as modern people, compartmentalized our lives to this great degree."],[386.25,"Where people have their quote unquote sex life that is this private thing off in a corner, has nothing to do with church good."],[396.94,"That's what St."],[397.66,"Paul was trying to tell them in Corinth."],[399.1,"It shouldn't have anything to do with church."],[400.78,"But has nothing to do with any of these other things."],[403.76,"We have it all sort of blocked off."],[405.62,"And so a lot of modern people then, you know, why is the church telling people, you know, why are they obsessed with sex?"],[413.02,"Why are they telling people about their sex lives?"],[414.78,"Why are they da da da da da."],[416.44,"So in the ancient world, life was not so compartmentalized."],[421.42,"This was not like this private thing that was between you and some romantic partner who you were in love with and making mixtapes for."],[431.43,"This was this was part of the public."],[434.65,"So this is taking place in public festivals."],[437.53,"This is taking place in religious rituals."],[441.61,"Um marriages of these things were public."],[445.37,"Uh, prostitution was completely legal."],[448.27,"This was this was not seen as this sort of other compartmentalized thing."],[453.01,"People were in arranged marriages, people didn't men, I shouldn't say people, men were not particularly faithful to those arranged marriages."],[461.93,"If the women weren't faithful, they would often have dire consequences if the husband found out about it."],[467.69,"Although with the Romans, sometimes the men knew about it and just didn't care because they were off doing their own thing or things too."],[477.66,"So this is part of why it's it's sort of a paradigmatic sin the way idolatry is."],[486.3,"Once you go down the road of sexual immorality or idolatry, all the other things are gonna come with it"],[493.03,"So if you're sexually immoral and you don't have any control over your sexual appetites, you're probably also gonna be a glutton."],[502.23,"You're probably also gonna be drinking too much alcohol."],[506.17,"Like these things kind of go together."],[508.03,"You're not gonna have, you know, well, I I have all of these things under perfect control about my physical appetites, except this one."],[515.4,"That's not gonna happen much."],[517.5,"And the same thing with idolatry."],[519.1,"Once you start engaging in these pagan festivals and doing these, these other things are gonna come along with it."],[525.44,"These other things are gonna come along with it."],[527.6,"And so when St."],[530.0,"Paul singles out sexual immorality or idolatry, it's not because, like, well, these are the really bad ones, and so if you're doing other stuff, that's not as bad."],[539.44,"Or I just gospel a little bit"],[540.14,"Like, I just gossip a little."],[541.58,"Like, I'm not like sexually immoral."],[545.4,"I'm not like worshiping idols."],[546.84,"That's not how it works."],[547.96,"Because we've seen when St."],[549.88,"Paul gives lists of sins, like refrain from all these things, he mixes those together."],[555.17,"He says sexual immorality, gossip, slander, brawling."],[559.59,"You know, he'll he lumps them all together."],[561.59,"So he's not saying these sins are worse."],[564.79,"They're paradigmatic because they they sort of lead to and exhibit all the others."],[572.89,"And they were particularly entrenched in the the Greco-Roman pagan world, uh, to which St."],[581.21,"Paul is writing and from which his pagan converts are coming, hopefully coming all the way out of."],[587.94,"Um so uh with as we saw with the Corinthians, a lot of the problem was that they were still entangled with the public feasts, eating at idol temples, doing these things."],[599.22,"We talked, I think last time about how becoming a Christian and leaving all of that idolatry and everything behind meant uh leaving your trade guild, leaving right your family associations, leaving all of the right, how much of a disjunction that was."],[617.28,"But Christianity was also teaching a very peculiar sexual morality for the ancient world."],[624.85,"For the ancient world."],[625.01,"the ancient world, Because the in um, how to put this delicately, Latin word that the Romans used for a man uh having sexual gratification is the same word as used for going to the bathroom."],[653.29,"Meaning they just saw it as a bodily function."],[656.83,"So you have to go to the bathroom."],[658.51,"They didn't have indoor plumbing in the ancient world."],[660.59,"You have to go to the bathroom, you go and find a receptacle."],[662.85,"You go and find a place to go to the bathroom."],[664.77,"They viewed for men, they viewed sexuality the same way."],[669.83,"Man wants to relieve his sexual appetite, he goes and finds a receptacle."],[675.11,"That might be a prostitute, that might be a slave."],[678.39,"In the Greek world, like in Thessalonica, Pederasty was a social institution."],[684.23,"So that might be a young person as young as eight years old."],[688.39,"Um there was no concept of consent for the other party."],[694.87,"Um and this was just common."],[698.87,"They didn't think about this."],[701.19,"No one thought this was odd."],[703.41,"No one thought this was horrible."],[706.85,"And then Christians came along, and said, none of that."],[712.17,"one of that N."],[713.29,"Not even polygamy"],[718.95,"It's it's you and your spouse, and that's it."],[722.55,"And we don't care that you didn't get to choose your spouse."],[728.07,"And so uh that was bizarre."],[731.03,"That was difficult for these early pagan converts to wrap their head around."],[736.33,"That was like a massive, you know, uh shift for them morally."],[741.93,"Uh later on, when you get into the second and third centuries, you find Roman writers who think that Christians are like superhuman because of this."],[753.53,"They think Christian men must be superhuman to practice this sexual morality because they can't imagine not just gratifying themselves whenever they wanted to."],[763.31,"So the Stoics and stuff are like the self-control is amazing."],[766.81,"Um, but at this stage, when we're talking about 50 AD, it's just a bunch of weirdos."],[778.21,"Not necessarily. Um, I mean, obviously, the sexual connected with idolatry, but uh the the Judeans at this time were still practicing polygamy and very easy divorce."],[793.35,"Divorce was I write a writ of divorce and hand it to my wife, and she's not my wife anymore."],[798.39,"Uh I go marry someone else, uh who's maybe younger and whatever."],[804.05,"Uh, and then do the same to her in a few years."],[806.77,"Um, yeah, so so Christianity even tightened that."],[814.79,"Even tightened that a great deal compared to what it got before."],[819.59,"And umce you get to the end of the first and beginning of the second century, Christianity is even holding out virginity and preserving it."],[831.27,"Not just for women, but for men."],[833.83,"Bizarro world. As an ideal."],[839.78,"Which uh, yeah, again. bizarro world if you're a pagan"],[850.34,"Okay, so this is this is then because of the way the culture was just entrenched in sexual immorality."],[859.32,"In Thessalonica, um this is gonna be one of the big ways that they're gonna need to, again, we're talking about sanctification being holy, being set apart."],[869.4,"This is one of the major ways they're gonna have to differentiate themselves from their neighbors in how they live their life."],[877.16,"This is one of the ways in which their walk, is gonna be radically different than."],[882.76,"It's not just, well, that guy still prays to Zeus, and now I pray to Jesus, and otherwise it's pretty much the same."],[886.68,"'s It not the same."],[890.32,"This is a radical thing."],[895.12,"Verse 4, that each of you should know how to possess his own vessel in sanctification and honor."],[901.52,"Uh possess his own vessel is uh uh little euphemistic."],[911.2,"But anyway, the idea is this is talking about self control."],[916.9,"This is talking about self-control."],[919.14,"Um it is, of course, primarily aimed at men because at this point it's only like adult men who are able to make these choices."],[930.74,"right Um it is good now that in our culture women have more agency and have a to consent, and that we don't allow that we have an age of consent."],[941.4,"And these things are all good."],[943.08,"That also means though that now that women have equal say, women now also have equal responsibility, to the commandments regarding morality."],[953.2,"So the reason they're directed at men, especially in the old testament, but even in the new testament, is because men were still the ones making the decisions."],[961.52,"And so what happened a lot in the ancient world is that even though women weren't allowed to make any of these decisions, they would then be blamed for the behavior."],[974.24,"So there's some examples of this in the Bible"],[977.0,"So the story of Susanna, which is the first chapter of Daniel in the Greek uh Old Testament tradition, uh, is about this Jewish woman who uh some of the elders of the people try to seduce her in private, she refuses, and they say, Well, okay, we're gonna tell everybody that you slept with us then, and you're gonna be, you know, stoned to death for being, you know, a prostitute."],[1003.62,"And Daniel has to kind of step in, and it ends up being those elders who are condemned and for it."],[1008.75,"But their attempt is, our behavior, but we're gonna blame them."],[1013.0,"And then, of course, probably the the most well-known example is the story of the woman caught in adultery. that traditionally gets put into the middle of St"],[1020.56,"John's gospel. Where, you know, oh, we've we caught this woman committing adultery, so we're gonna stone her to death."],[1027.89,"Was she committing adultery by herself?"],[1033.17,"Where is the guy?"],[1035.43,"And if you read the commandments about that in Deuteronomy, the guy gets stoned to death no matter what."],[1043.43,"The woman may or may not, depending on the circumstances, but the man is always punished for it."],[1049.51,"Because again, in the Torah, since men are the ones making the decisions, they have the primary responsibility."],[1056.89,"But what we find, and what Jesus has to step in to correct, is that even though it was some man making the decision to commit adultery with her, she's the one they're trying to punish."],[1071.46,"And so that's why the commandments are phrased the way they are."],[1077.7,"Sometimes uh modern people read, especially the old testament, and they say, Well, this is talking about women like their children and can't make their own decisions, and it's like that's not the Bible doing that to them, that's the culture did that to them."],[1093.0,"Their fathers and brothers and sons did that to them."],[1096.14,"And God is stepping into that and saying, Well, okay, if you're not gonna let them make their own decisions."],[1102.56,"If you're gonna make all the decisions, then you're gonna be held completely responsible for those decisions."],[1104.56,"And not them"],[1109.78,"You're not going to not let them make decisions and then blame them for what happens."],[1115.95,"It's not, it's not going to work that way."],[1119.31,"But so that's why, I mean, I'm not going to go into details of what the language about controlling his own vessel."],[1125.15,"One can guess."],[1125.95,"Yeah, possessing your own vessel."],[1127.55,"You can guess."],[1129.55,"It's sanctification, honor, in holiness and honor being set apart."],[1135.28,"And showing honor, to the person with whom you do use that vessel."],[1144.02,"And that's opposed to."],[1145.4,"So sanctification and honor is opposed to. in verse 5, not in passion of lust, like the Gentiles who do not know God"],[1154.0,"Like the nations who do not know God."],[1156.34,"And this is a theme St."],[1157.7,"Paul's picking up."],[1158.5,"Remember, all the way back in chapter 1, he was talking about them coming to now know God."],[1162.26,"They know God and God knows them."],[1164.35,"So St. Paul's saying, yeah, those your neighbors who still don't know God, who haven't come to know God in Christ, yeah, they're still following around their passions, following around their lust, just trying to gratify their desires."],[1178.69,"You can't be like that."],[1180.45,"Because now you do know God."],[1182.85,"And so now you need to be holy and set apart."],[1188.77,"Verse 6: that no one should take advantage. of and defraud his brother in this matter"],[1193.85,"Because the Lord is the avenger of all such, as we also forewarned you and testified."],[1201.05,"So this, if you don't take it in context, uh, it could be hard to interpret."],[1207.98,"But notice he says defraud in this matter."],[1212.4,"So he's still talking about sexual immorality."],[1214.8,"So what's he talking about?"],[1215.92,"How would you defraud your brother in sexual immorality?"],[1220.8,"Sleeping with his daughter."],[1223.12,"Sleeping with his wife."],[1226.46,"And notice we're still using the masculine terms."],[1229.27,"Because it's the men making the decisions."],[1235.09,"So the the use of uh defraud here, means what's what's fraud in terms of the old testament is usually it's referred to as false measures."],[1250.61,"Somebody comes to you to buy a certain amount of grain and you give them less than that."],[1254.87,"You put a you have something that's a little light on the scale, to put on the other side and be like, oh yeah, here's your right, and you cheat them."],[1262.02,"You're either you're either taking something that you aren't entitled to from them, or uh you're giving them less than what they're entitled to."],[1276.61,"So if we take that into the area of sexual morality, if you're not gonna def if no one defrauds his brother, what St."],[1286.48,"Paul is saying is you're not going to be taking anything that you don't have rights to."],[1292.26,"You're not gonna be going and taking anything from anyone."],[1294.82,"So this would include children, slaves, women who aren't your wife, etc."],[1300.28,"etc. Uh, and you're also see the flip side of that then is you're also not going to be giving someone, namely your wife, less than what she is entitled to."],[1315.45,"Which is faithfulness, honor, etc"],[1322.61,"And why? Because the Lord is the avenger of all such, as we also forewarned you and testified."],[1331.19,"So this is this is St."],[1333.11,"Paul. He qualified this as commandments."],[1335.52,"This is coming straight from the Torah."],[1338.02,"This is where the laws about sexual morality are, Leviticus 18."],[1342.76,"And he's even drawing the language from the Torah of why."],[1346.46,"Because if you take advantage of someone."],[1349.12,"If you abuse someone who's in this weaker or socially powerless position."],[1355.57,"So who has no one to stick up for them, Yahweh, the God of Israel is going to stick up for them."],[1358.61,"you That's not going to go well for."],[1367.42,"You think if you go and take someone some woman's virginity, her dad's going to be mad at you."],[1373.74,"If she doesn't have a dad and it's God."],[1379.07,"You're not going to have a good time."],[1380.72,"So this is um and this is in a this is an important, this is an important aspect that we forget about in terms of the way justice is talked about in the Torah."],[1393.78,"That God isn't uh God isn't actually impartial."],[1401.66,"God takes sides."],[1403.66,"He takes the side of the weak, the poor, the powerless, the people who have no one to defend them."],[1409.59,"He's on their side."],[1411.85,"He's he's not just even-handed."],[1413.69,"He's on their side."],[1415.26,"Um, and so this warning comes again and again in the Torah, and St."],[1421.18,"Paul refers to it here and says, This is what I taught you before while I was there."],[1426.99,"So we just read these two paragraphs."],[1432.11,"Say Paul is teaching pagans who convert."],[1438.61,"Pagans who become Christians, that they need to follow the commandments of the Torah about sexual morality."],[1443.01,"This is so St"],[1447.69,"Paul, when he defends himself, in the book of Acts, as we saw again and again, the Jewish accusation, oh, you're teaching people not to follow the law, you're blaspheming against Moses, all that that he got over and over and over again."],[1459.85,"It's not just him trying to do some tricks like, oh no, no, I don't get rid of the law, I establish it, but I really kind of get rid of it."],[1467.09,"That's not just sophistry."],[1470.5,"He actually is."],[1473.08,"He actually is."],[1474.04,"He's not going to these Greeks in Thessalonica and saying, oh yeah, just stop worshiping those gods, start worshiping Jesus, and you're good."],[1483.4,"It's not at all what he's teaching them."],[1486.66,"He's teaching them a radical holiness that's based on the commandments from the Torah, specifically the holiness code."],[1494.6,"He's telling them how to be holy is to follow the holiness code in Leviticus."],[1505.64,"Verse 7, but God did not call us to uncleanness, but in holiness."],[1511.72,"Therefore, he who does not he who rejects this does not reject man but God, who has also given us his Holy Spirit."],[1520.52,"Okay So again, he uses, notice what he contrasts with holiness is uncleanness."],[1529.4,"So this is Torah, Leviticus. language again"],[1532.81,"He's still using this language, even though he's talking to former pagans."],[1537.83,"God didn't call us to stay unclean, to just keep living the way we were living."],[1544.41,"But change who in our head, we think about when we pray."],[1548.61,"But called us to holiness."],[1552.61,"And so if you reject this idea, you say, ah nah, it doesn't matter."],[1557.82,"Just like going to the bathroom."],[1559.72,"Then you're not just rejecting like some rules St."],[1564.04,"Paul tried to make you follow."],[1566.02,"You're not rejecting somebody's idea of morality from some philosopher."],[1570.56,"You're rejecting. the Holy Spirit that he gave you and put inside of you"],[1570.68,"You're rejecting God Himself."],[1577.16,"Because you can't have the Holy Spirit dwelling inside of you and be unclean."],[1582.92,"Doesn't work. Because what happens with the Holy Spirit."],[1587.59,"What happened to Ananias and Sapphira when they came and lied to the Holy Spirit?"],[1591.91,"When the Holy Spirit was around and they were unclean, they fell over dead."],[1595.73,"Happens all the time in the Old Testament."],[1598.21,"Somebody is doing something unclean, somebody unclean comes up and touches the Ark of the Covenant."],[1603.31,"Or goes into the tabernacle."],[1605.39,"They fall over dead."],[1607.84,"You got that option."],[1609.52,"Or when all of Judah became unclean. God got up from the temple and left"],[1618.85,"So if you have the Holy Spirit dwelling within you, St."],[1622.37,"Paul's saying, and you're gonna go and be unclean, either, like you warned the Corinthians with the Eucharist, you're gonna die."],[1631.17,"Or he's gonna leave."],[1635.33,"Because he's holy, and so if you're not, he can't dwell inside of you."],[1647.01,"Verse 9. So that was sort of the the negative."],[1650.62,"Don't do this."],[1652.0,"Now, this is gonna be the positive."],[1654.0,"But concerning brotherly love, you have no need that I should write to you."],[1656.4,"For you yourselves are taught by God to love one another"],[1660.76,"And indeed you do so toward all the brethren who are in all Macedonia."],[1666.36,"But we urge you, brethren, that you increase more and more."],[1671.8,"So we say the positive of this."],[1674.38,"The opposite of you trying to take advantage of each other to gratify your desires."],[1679.32,"That kind of lust and sexuality is actual love."],[1683.82,"Which is giving rather than taking."],[1686.87,"Which is caring rather than trying to gratify yourself, you're trying to gratify someone else."],[1691.03,"And he says, I don't have to explain this to you, you"],[1693.55,"You're already doing this."],[1695.53,"And remember there that Thessalonica is the metropolis, it's the capital of the province of Macedonia."],[1698.89,"So he's saying the whole province of Macedonia knows you're there in the capital"],[1705.17,"They know about you and how your community is."],[1708.29,"He says, But I want you to not just keep it up, but more and more and more."],[1713.27,"More of the good stuff, less of the bad stuff."],[1717.75,"That you also aspire to lead a quiet life, to mind your own business, and to work with your own hands as we commanded you."],[1727.19,"So this is St."],[1729.03,"Paul, right here."],[1729.83,"Again, this is the positive."],[1731.21,"Not just the negative."],[1732.09,"Don't do the commandments, don't do this, thou shalt not."],[1734.97,"But here's the positive."],[1736.69,"This is not uh the good life as we modern Americans conceive of it."],[1743.75,"Our society does not tell you that you should aspire to have a quiet life in which you mind your own business and work with your own hands."],[1753.43,"No, it doesn't."],[1756.71,"Uh everybody wants to avoid uh well, working as much as possible, but uh especially working with your own hands."],[1765.51,"Yeah. If you could get out of that, if you could get to the point where you get paid a lot of money just to shove numbers around a spreadsheet in a cubicle."],[1772.11,"Well, that's an improvement over working with your hands."],[1774.83,"And who wants to live a quiet life."],[1776.61,"I want to be famous and I want to get rich."],[1779.38,"We want to make as much noise as we possibly can."],[1781.43,"Yeah, more more and more and more."],[1783.91,"This is Greek pleonyxia"],[1787.29,"It used to be, it used to be considered a vice for most of Christian history."],[1793.13,"But in our modern culture, it's considered a virtue."],[1797.31,"Wanting more. I want more."],[1801.17,"So, to anyone, which is probably everyone, if we're honest, probably everyone in this room, and probably everyone who will listen to this recording later, uh, who thinks to themselves, my life would be better or happier, or even perfect if I had just like 10% more than what I've got now."],[1819.25,"Then everything would be boom."],[1821.41,"I have good news and I have bad news for you."],[1824.69,"The bad news is it wouldn't."],[1829.27,"If you had 10% more than what you have now, you would think, if I just had 10% more than this, then everything, and that would continue forever."],[1840.8,"So that's the bad news."],[1842.24,"The good news is you could be just as happy with what you have now as you could be with 10% more."],[1853.28,"Because the problem isn't that we don't have quite enough."],[1857.28,"The problem is that we always want more."],[1861.12,"That's the problem."],[1864.34,"That's the problem."],[1866.9,"And, you know, wh whileile there have been greedy people and stuff in all ages of humanity, and people who wanted more, this is a particular, this should be a particularly countercultural thing for us as modern slash postmodern people in Western capitalist democracies."],[1892.62,"Where while our whole culture constantly, I mean, ad how much of our culture is advertising now?"],[1900.86,"Just about everything is an advertisement."],[1903.98,"You know, uh is constantly aimed at telling us, no, you just need more, a little more."],[1911.18,"Just a little more."],[1912.58,"Uh, we as Christians should be the countercultural ones who are saying, nah, I got enough."],[1916.06,"I'm thankful for all this that I've got"],[1920.02,"I got more than enough."],[1923.54,"In fact, I could probably get rid of some of this and be just as happy with a little less."],[1930.2,"This is this is a place where we as Christians could be sending a powerful message to our friends and neighbors about the difference between the Christian way of life and the world's way of life."],[1947.21,"Uh fortunately, after 2,000 years of Christianity, as I was just talking about, we view women differently, and we view children differently, and we view slavery differently, and we view sexuality differently."],[1959.79,"Uh, and those are all good."],[1961.79,"And so that means, although our society's not moving in a great direction on any of that either, uh, at least for the at least for the nonce, uh, that may be less of a temptation than it was for the people St."],[1975.63,"Paul is writing to at Thessalonica."],[1977.71,"Where it would be very easy to fall into that kind of into a kind of sexual immorality that we think is disgusting now."],[1985.4,"Even an atheist would think was disgusting now, uh, but was commonplace and wouldn't have batted an eye at in Thessalonica."],[1993.02,"So we we've got less temptation there."],[1995.02,"But I think we've got much more temptation to the quiet and peaceful life."],[2001.42,"Work with my hands."],[2003.26,"Aspire to that"],[2008.42,"That may be where you start out, but you want to get out of that as fast as you possibly can."],[2012.72,"Um this is a place where I think we're called to be more uh counter-cultural."],[2022.88,"More countercultural. Um and the working the the the working with our hands part is important because uh I don't know anybody who sits around and fantasizes and says, Wow, I could really hone a craft and become really good at it."],[2044.24,"And gradually, over time, by selling the things that I craft. I could have a quiet and decent life"],[2053.14,"Usually people are scheming about, well, here's how I can get a lot of money really fast."],[2059.59,"And people are out there to tell you, oh, hey, I have the way that you could get a lot of money really fast."],[2065.51,"And so people fall prey to those people and you know, end up getting defrauded over and over again."],[2072.16,"Because that's what's attractive to us."],[2074.0,"I want to win the publisher's clearing house sweepstakes."],[2077.92,"I don't want to work hard and have a nice life."],[2080.8,"I want instant, instant gratification."],[2085.5,"And that's what St."],[2086.62,"Paul means by working with your hands."],[2091.08,"He means, no, you find a decent, honorable, honest way to support yourself and your family."],[2097.72,"And you work hard at it."],[2100.36,"And you have a nice life."],[2104.68,"And you follow and you follow Christ."],[2108.41,"I think the older you get, the better it sounds."],[2113.21,"I'm a very elderly man at heart, so it sounds good to me too."],[2116.89,"But when you're in your 20s, this definitely does not sound"],[2124.58,"Once you get to a certain age, you've seen enough of the world and you don't want any more excitement."],[2128.59,"But at a certain age, you kind of do want the excitement."],[2134.15,"But so yeah, that this is the vision, the positive vision that Saint Paul puts forward."],[2142.25,"Of quiet, decency, hard work, holiness, honor."],[2149.55,"That's what he's setting forward."],[2150.83,"Over against chasing after gratifying your desires."],[2156.69,"Whether it's pride, wanting to be well known and loved by people, whether it's envy and wanting more and more and more and more and more greed, or whether it's lust."],[2170.07,"Gluttony, all of those things."],[2172.55,"There's this. This is what St."],[2174.79,"Paul is. setting before them"],[2182.99,"Verse 12, that you may walk properly toward those who are outside, and that you may lack nothing."],[2190.03,"So that toward those who are outside."],[2194.71,"That's again the point."],[2197.51,"Is that you're holy in the sense that you're set apart and you're different."],[2202.71,"You're different. Because if the people outside the church look at the people who are in the church, and they seem pretty much the same, there's not a lot motivating them to come to Christ."],[2219.11,"I've heard them say that's why."],[2225.23,"I could, if if we're both gonna live the same way and have the same problems and have the same everything, then I'll stay home Sunday and watch football in my underpants."],[2235.01,"Like save myself some time and effort."],[2237.99,"This would be different."],[2239.35,"And and why is this such a big issue."],[2244.09,"Well, what was the purpose of Israel in the Old Testament?"],[2247.53,"Israel wasn't chosen because God loved them and he hated all the other nations and and was going to send them all to hell and Israel was all going to go to heaven."],[2256.19,"If you think that you haven't read much of the Old Testament."],[2259.27,"That's the the whole point was that God was going to save all the nations. through Israel"],[2266.01,"City on a hill, light to the world."],[2269.63,"All of that stuff."],[2270.93,"Well, Christ says all of that stuff about the church."],[2275.3,"So it isn't that."],[2278.35,"And people get this wrong."],[2279.47,"And a lot of times when we talk to our friends who are Christians but aren't Orthodox Christians, who aren't part of the Orthodox Church, and they hear us say, for example, that the Orthodox Church is the true church."],[2293.09,"This is the one true church."],[2294.85,"They don't like that."],[2295.81,"Because what they're hearing is: well, God loves you guys, and you guys are all going to heaven, and the rest of us, he's all gonna send to hell."],[2304.77,"What St Which, of course, isn't what we're saying either."],[2311.35,"Paul is saying to these Orthodox Christians in Thessalonica and in AD 50 is you are the means by which God wants to save the people of Thessalonica."],[2327.99,"God loves your pagan neighbor just as much as he loves you."],[2332.63,"Because you're in the church, because you're in the community, you have this responsibility."],[2338.55,"You're going to be the one through whom God shows his love to that pagan."],[2344.63,"You're going to be the one through whom he calls that pagan to change his life and repent. and come and join the church"],[2353.63,"And for that to happen."],[2355.68,"For that to happen, you've got to be on the right path."],[2360.8,"You've got to be on the right path."],[2362.72,"So what St."],[2364.72,"Paul is saying here is that even when it comes to salvation, we can't be selfish."],[2371.38,"St. Paul is telling them you need to walk the right path."],[2374.9,"You need to find salvation."],[2376.74,"Because there are all these people who God wants to save through you."],[2383.46,"You need to do it for their sake."],[2386.91,"Not for your own."],[2388.27,"So even there, it's not about like, hey, you don't want to go to hell, do you?"],[2394.09,"That's no, God's called you and given you this mission and this purpose, and you need to succeed at it."],[2399.61,"Because again, if you've read the Old Testament, you see what happens to people who God calls to do that and they fail."],[2407.69,"They're not any better off than those pagans."],[2411.61,"In fact, kind of worse off."],[2414.09,"They're more responsible."],[2418.41,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2425.77,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2431.13,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.96,"lv":2,"h":"Opening - the beginning of the end","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>1 Thess 4:1</b> - begins \"<b>Finally, then</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"beginning of the end\" - like \"let us complete our prayer unto the Lord\": still <b>a couple chapters</b> left</li><li class=\"d0\">So far one of Paul's <b>more positive letters</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Corinthians didn't go this well - more like <b>Philippians</b>, good things happening</li><li class=\"d0\">Report back from <b>Timothy</b>; Paul writing ~<b>50 AD from Corinth</b>, hasn't been there since first visit</li><li class=\"d0\">Summary: encouraging them to <b>keep walking</b> the way he showed</li><li class=\"d1\">not just what he taught, but <b>how he lived</b> there, how they saw Timothy live</li><li class=\"d1\">\"keep up the good work\", don't get distracted or go off course</li></ul>"},{"t":176.36,"lv":2,"h":"4:1-2 - Abound more and more; commandments","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:1</b>: \"urge and exhort in the Lord Jesus\" - abound <b>more and more</b> in how to <b>walk and please God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">summarizing last chapter and a half: \"more of the good stuff, less of the bad\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v2</b>: \"you know what <b>commandments</b> we gave you through the Lord Jesus\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul gave <b>commandments</b> - links to the Gospels</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Matthew</b> end: \"teaching them all that <b>I have commanded you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"If you love me, <b>you will keep my commandments</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Past the epistles about <b>law</b> - but to hammer it home</li><li class=\"d1\">church full of <b>Gentiles</b>, former pagans → he teaches <b>commandments</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just \"here's what to <b>believe</b>, then you'll be fine\"</li></ul>"},{"t":284.49,"lv":2,"h":"4:3 - Sanctification: abstain from sexual immorality","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v3</b>: \"this is the will of God, your <b>sanctification</b>: abstain from <b>sexual immorality</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"because\" - expanding on the commandments</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sanctification</b> = becoming <b>holy, set apart</b>, different from pagan neighbors</li><li class=\"d0\">Corinth: mostly <b>idolatry</b>, some sexual immorality (\"everything going on\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Two <b>paradigmatic sins</b> of the nations, as Judeans saw it: <b>sexual immorality and idolatry</b></li><li class=\"d1\">closely connected all through the <b>OT</b></li><li class=\"d1\">pagan worship and <b>festivals</b> involved sexual immorality</li></ul>"},{"t":367.81,"lv":3,"h":"Compartmentalized moderns vs public ancient life","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moderns <b>compartmentalize</b>: \"sex life\" private, off in a corner</li><li class=\"d1\">so: \"why is the church <b>obsessed with sex</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient world not like that - not \"private thing between you and a partner you're making <b>mixtapes</b> for\"</li><li class=\"d1\">in <b>public festivals</b>, <b>religious rituals</b>, public <b>marriages</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>prostitution completely legal</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>arranged marriages</b>: men not faithful; women unfaithful faced <b>dire consequences</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Romans: sometimes men knew and <b>didn't care</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why paradigmatic: go down that road, <b>all others come with it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no control of sexual appetites → probably <b>glutton, drunk</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same with idolatry: festivals bring other things along</li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"the really bad ones, so <b>gossip</b> is fine\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's lists <b>mix them</b>: sexual immorality, <b>gossip, slander, brawling</b></li><li class=\"d1\">paradigmatic because they <b>lead to and exhibit</b> the others - entrenched in <b>Greco-Roman</b> world</li></ul>"},{"t":587.94,"lv":3,"h":"Christianity's peculiar sexual morality","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Corinthians: still in <b>public feasts</b>, <b>idol temples</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Becoming Christian = leaving <b>trade guild, family associations</b> - huge disjunction</li><li class=\"d0\">Also very <b>peculiar sexual morality</b> for the ancient world</li><li class=\"d1\">Latin word for man's gratification = <b>same as going to the bathroom</b> - a <b>bodily function</b></li><li class=\"d1\">man finds \"a <b>receptacle</b>\" - <b>prostitute, slave</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Greek world / Thessalonica: <b>pederasty</b> a social institution - child <b>as young as eight</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no concept of consent</b>; \"no one thought this was odd\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Christians: \"<b>none of that</b>\" - not even <b>polygamy</b>: <b>you and your spouse</b>, and that's it</li><li class=\"d1\">even if you <b>didn't choose</b> your spouse</li><li class=\"d0\">Massive moral shift for converts</li><li class=\"d1\">2nd-3rd c.: Roman writers think Christians <b>superhuman</b>; Stoics: \"self-control is amazing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">at 50 AD: \"a bunch of <b>weirdos</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":778.21,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: Judean practice, divorce, virginity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: was it also unlike Judean practice?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Judeans</b> still practiced <b>polygamy</b> and <b>easy divorce</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>writ of divorce</b>, wife \"not my wife anymore\", marry someone younger, repeat</li><li class=\"d0\">Christianity <b>tightened</b> that a great deal</li><li class=\"d0\">End of 1st / start of 2nd c.: even holds up <b>virginity</b> - <b>for men too</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>bizarro world</b>\" if you're a pagan</li><li class=\"d0\">So one major way Thessalonians must <b>differentiate</b> from neighbors</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"that guy prays to <b>Zeus</b>, I pray to <b>Jesus</b>, otherwise same\" - <b>radical</b></li></ul>"},{"t":895.12,"lv":2,"h":"4:4 - Possess his own vessel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v4</b>: \"know how to <b>possess his own vessel</b> in sanctification and <b>honor</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a little <b>euphemistic</b>\" - means <b>self-control</b></li><li class=\"d1\">aimed at <b>men</b> - only adult men making these choices</li><li class=\"d0\">Good now: women have <b>agency, consent</b>, <b>age of consent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also means <b>equal responsibility</b> for the commandments</li><li class=\"d0\">Directed at men, OT and NT, because <b>men made the decisions</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in ancient world women made none, yet were <b>blamed</b></li></ul>"},{"t":977.0,"lv":3,"h":"Susanna and the woman caught in adultery","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Susanna</b> (Daniel 1, Greek OT): elders try to <b>seduce her</b>, she refuses → \"we'll say you slept with us\" → <b>stoned as prostitute</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Daniel</b> steps in → <b>elders condemned</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Woman caught in adultery</b> (John): \"stone her\" - \"was she committing adultery by herself? <b>Where is the guy?</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Deuteronomy</b>: man <b>stoned no matter what</b>; woman maybe, by circumstances</li><li class=\"d1\">Torah: men decide → <b>primary responsibility</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus corrects: they punish her</li><li class=\"d0\">Moderns say OT treats women \"like children\" - \"<b>not the Bible, the culture did that</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">fathers, brothers, sons did</li><li class=\"d1\">God: \"if you make <b>all the decisions</b>, you're <b>completely responsible</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not going to not let them decide and then <b>blame them</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1119.31,"lv":2,"h":"4:5 - Not in passion of lust, like the Gentiles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Vessel\" language - \"one can guess\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sanctification and honor</b> = set apart; <b>honor</b> to the person with whom you use it</li><li class=\"d0\">Opposed to <b>v5</b>: \"not in <b>passion of lust</b>, like the <b>Gentiles who do not know God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Theme from <b>chapter 1</b>: they have come to <b>know God</b>, God knows them</li><li class=\"d1\">neighbors still follow <b>passions</b>, gratify desires</li><li class=\"d1\">\"can't be like that - <b>now you do know God</b>\" → <b>holy and set apart</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1188.77,"lv":2,"h":"4:6 - Do not defraud his brother","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v6</b>: \"no one should take advantage of and <b>defraud his brother in this matter</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"in this matter\" = still <b>sexual immorality</b></li><li class=\"d1\">defraud = <b>sleeping with his daughter, his wife</b>; masculine terms again - men decide</li><li class=\"d0\">OT fraud = <b>false measures</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sell grain, give <b>less</b>; light on the scale</li><li class=\"d1\">taking what you're not entitled to / <b>giving less than owed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">In sexual morality: not taking anything you <b>have no rights to</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>children, slaves, women not your wife</b></li><li class=\"d1\">flip side: not giving your <b>wife</b> less than she's owed - <b>faithfulness, honor</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1322.61,"lv":3,"h":"\"The Lord is the avenger\" - God takes sides","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Lord is <b>avenger of all such</b>, as we forewarned\" - <b>commandments straight from Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sexual morality laws = <b>Leviticus 18</b>; Torah language for <b>why</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Abuse someone <b>weak, socially powerless</b> → <b>Yahweh</b> sticks up for them</li><li class=\"d1\">take a woman's virginity: <b>her dad</b> mad; <b>no dad</b>? → <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Often forgotten in Torah justice: <b>God isn't impartial</b></li><li class=\"d1\">takes side of <b>weak, poor, powerless</b>, \"no one to defend them\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not just even-handed - on their side\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So Paul teaches <b>pagan converts</b> to follow <b>Torah</b> commandments on sexual morality</li><li class=\"d1\">Acts: accused of <b>teaching against the law</b>, blaspheming Moses</li><li class=\"d1\">not <b>sophistry</b> (\"I establish the law but really get rid of it\")</li><li class=\"d1\">teaches <b>radical holiness</b> based on the <b>holiness code, Leviticus</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1505.64,"lv":2,"h":"4:7-8 - Called to holiness, not uncleanness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v7</b>: \"God did not call us to <b>uncleanness</b>, but in <b>holiness</b>\" - <b>Leviticus</b> language to former pagans</li><li class=\"d1\">didn't call us to stay unclean, \"change who in our head we think about when we pray\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v8</b>: reject this → not man but <b>God</b>, who gave <b>his Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just Paul's rules or a philosopher's morality</li><li class=\"d1\">\"can't have the <b>Holy Spirit</b> dwelling in you and be <b>unclean</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Ananias and Sapphira: lied to the Spirit → <b>fell over dead</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT: unclean touches the <b>Ark</b> or enters <b>tabernacle</b> → dead</li><li class=\"d1\">all of Judah unclean → <b>God got up from the temple and left</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Holy Spirit in you + unclean → like <b>Corinthians and the Eucharist</b>: <b>die</b>, or <b>he leaves</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1647.01,"lv":2,"h":"4:9-10 - Brotherly love, more and more","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Negative done; now <b>positive</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v9</b>: \"concerning <b>brotherly love</b> you have no need I write - <b>taught by God</b> to love one another\"</li><li class=\"d1\">opposite of <b>lust / taking advantage</b> = real <b>love</b>: <b>giving rather than taking</b>, gratifying <b>someone else</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Already do it toward all brethren in <b>all Macedonia</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Thessalonica = <b>capital of Macedonia</b> - whole province knows their community</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v10</b>: \"increase <b>more and more</b>\" - \"more of the good stuff, less of the bad\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1717.75,"lv":2,"h":"4:11 - A quiet life, minding your business, working with your hands","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v11</b>: \"aspire to lead a <b>quiet life</b>, <b>mind your own business</b>, <b>work with your own hands</b> as we commanded\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The positive, not just \"thou shalt not\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> the good life as modern Americans see it</li><li class=\"d1\">everyone wants to avoid working with hands: \"paid a lot to shove numbers around a spreadsheet in a <b>cubicle</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"who wants a quiet life? I want to be <b>famous</b> and get <b>rich</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Greek <i>pleonexia</i> = wanting more</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>vice</b> for most of Christian history; now considered a <b>virtue</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1801.17,"lv":3,"h":"The 10% more - contentment","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"My life would be better, even perfect, if I had <b>10% more</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>bad news</b>: it wouldn't - you'd want 10% more again, forever</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>good news</b>: you could be <b>just as happy with what you have</b></li><li class=\"d1\">problem isn't not enough - \"<b>we always want more</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Greed in all ages, but especially <b>countercultural</b> for moderns</li><li class=\"d1\">culture = <b>advertising</b>: \"just a little more\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Christians should say \"<b>I got enough</b>\", \"thankful\", \"could get rid of some of this and be <b>just as happy</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"powerful message\" on <b>Christian way vs world's way</b></li><li class=\"d0\">After 2,000 years we view <b>women, children, slavery, sexuality</b> differently - good</li><li class=\"d1\">less temptation to ancient sexual immorality (even <b>atheists</b> find it disgusting now)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>more</b> temptation against the quiet and peaceful life</li></ul>"},{"t":2003.26,"lv":3,"h":"Working with our hands","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Aspire\" - may be where you start, but \"get out of that as fast as you can\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Nobody fantasizes: <b>hone a craft</b>, get really good, sell what I make, have a <b>quiet and decent life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">scheming instead: \"<b>get a lot of money really fast</b>\" → people <b>defrauded over and over</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>Publishers Clearing House</b> sweepstakes\" - want <b>instant gratification</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul means: <b>decent, honorable, honest way</b> to support yourself and family</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>work hard</b>, have a nice life, <b>follow Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"The older you get, the better it sounds\" - \"very elderly man at heart\"; in your <b>20s</b> not so much</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's vision: <b>quiet, decency, hard work, holiness, honor</b></li><li class=\"d1\">over against gratifying desires: <b>pride</b> (being well known), <b>envy/greed</b>, <b>lust</b>, <b>gluttony</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2182.99,"lv":2,"h":"4:12 - Walk properly toward those outside","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v12</b>: \"walk properly toward <b>those who are outside</b>, and lack nothing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Point: <b>holy = set apart, different</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if outsiders see church people <b>pretty much the same</b> → little motivation to come</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'll <b>stay home Sunday and watch football in my underpants</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why it matters: <b>purpose of Israel</b> in OT</li><li class=\"d1\">not chosen because God loved them, hated others, <b>others to hell</b></li><li class=\"d1\">whole point: God would <b>save all the nations through Israel</b> - \"<b>city on a hill, light to the world</b>\"; Christ says it of the <b>church</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2279.47,"lv":3,"h":"The church as means of salvation for neighbors","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Non-Orthodox Christians hear \"Orthodox Church is the <b>one true church</b>\" as \"you're going to heaven, the rest of us <b>hell</b>\" - \"not what we're saying\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul to Thessalonians (50 AD): \"<b>you are the means by which God wants to save the people of Thessalonica</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God loves <b>pagan neighbor</b> as much as you</li><li class=\"d1\">you're the one through whom he shows love, calls to <b>repent and join the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so you've got to be on the <b>right path</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Even salvation <b>can't be selfish</b></li><li class=\"d1\">find salvation \"<b>for their sake, not your own</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"you don't want to go to <b>hell</b>, do you?\" - God gave you a <b>mission and purpose</b></li><li class=\"d0\">OT: people called who <b>failed</b> → \"not better off than those pagans - <b>worse off, more responsible</b>\"</li></ul>"}]});
